iables approximated at the cell centers", "meaning": "cell-center location subscript; distinguish this from hydraulic head H"}]
note "For GWHEADMODEL_PARABOLIC, H=hbc-(2/3)*gwcurv*gwheight² exactly supplies the written expanded x-gradient. Horizontal velocity then uses -Kin*headgrad, multiplied by interface heights. Differences: Written y-gradient repeats the i+1,j neighbor, while code uses i,j+1. An exponential head option uses sinh/cosh expressions instead of the parabolic expansion. gwnonh selects the groundwater pressure path (:373-382). gw_solver receives Kxupd/Kyupd/Kzupd (:382); gw_calculate_velocities receives separate Kx/Ky/Kz inputs (:397), reset to par%kx/par%ky/par%kz after each step (:415-417). Inside that routine Kxupd/Kyupd/Kzupd are intent(out) (:1287) and are overwritten with Kx/Ky/Kz (:1303-1305). Its Kin arguments are Kx/Ky/Kz (:1311-1329), not the conductivity supplied to the solver. isnonh is supplied by par%wavemodel==WAVEMODEL_NONH, independently of gwnonh. Laminar horizontal velocity uses Kin; turbulent horizontal velocity uses Kin in that surface-wave nonhydrostatic mode and otherwise its locally recomputed Kupd (:1429-1433). Interface heights and dry-cell caps modify the fluxes. Open: B.21/B.23 define the Δz/q_gw symbols; the y-neighbor discrepancy remains unresolved. Definition locators refer only to explicit naming/defining clauses; no defining meaning is inferred from introductory prose. Unbound exact notation uses undefined_in_document. Groundwater physical η and η_gw definitions are cited; any ζ, δ/d, H_gw/H or q pressure/flux variants remain distinct as recorded. Association object top-level member is null as in the packet adapter shape; the actual original member, relationship, locator, preview hashes and OLE hash are preserved in the nested object. S1 correction: q_gw^x, q_gw^y, Δz_u, Δz_v now link to explicit defining clauses/equations; formerly claimed missing definitions for these symbols are withdrawn. Remaining gaps concern the literal variants listed under search_scope.symbol_definitions and the stated model/implementation issues. S3: gwnonh selects the groundwater pressure path (:373-382). gw_solver receives Kxupd/Kyupd/Kzupd (:382); gw_calculate_velocities receives separate Kx/Ky/Kz inputs (:397), reset to par%kx/par%ky/par%kz after each step (:415-417). Inside that routine Kxupd/Kyupd/Kzupd are intent(out) (:1287) and are overwritten with Kx/Ky/Kz (:1303-1305). Its Kin arguments are Kx/Ky/Kz (:1311-1329), not the conductivity supplied to the solver. isnonh is supplied by par%wavemodel==WAVEMODEL_NONH, independently of gwnonh. Laminar horizontal velocity uses Kin; turbulent horizontal velocity uses Kin in that surface-wave nonhydrostatic mode and otherwise its locally recomputed Kupd (:1429-1433). Comment/code mismatch: groundwater.F90:387-396 claims velocity uses the Poisson-solver conductivity estimate. The actual call at :397 supplies base Kx/Ky/Kz as Kin, while :1287 declares updated arrays intent(out) and :1303-1305 overwrites them; therefore the incoming pressure-solver Kxupd/Kyupd/Kzupd estimate does not determine the velocity conductivity. The comments at :370 and :1430 make the same unsupported claim. Which conductivity best serves the intended coupled method and its conservation consequences are not resolved here."
closure_status "unresolved"
implementation_relation "mapped_with_differences"
